<h2 id="is-it-free">Is it free to publish a book on Amazon?</h2>
<p>Yes. <strong>Kindle Direct Publishing (KDP) charges nothing</strong> to create an account, upload your manuscript and cover, or keep your book on sale. That’s true for Kindle ebooks, paperbacks and hardcovers.</p>
<p>Here’s what you get for free on KDP:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Publishing and listing</strong> on Amazon stores worldwide.</li>
  <li><strong>A free ISBN</strong> for paperbacks and hardcovers (ebooks don’t need one; Amazon assigns an ASIN).</li>
  <li><strong>Print-on-demand</strong>: Amazon prints each paperback only when someone orders it.</li>
  <li><strong>Free tools</strong> such as Kindle Create for formatting and the cover creator.</li>
</ul>
<p>So where does Amazon make its money? From each sale. For ebooks, you keep a royalty of 35% or 70% depending on price and territory. For paperbacks, you keep 60% of the list price (at $9.99 and above on Amazon.com) <em>minus the printing cost</em>. We’ll run the numbers in the <a href="#royalties">royalties section</a>.</p>
<p>If you’d rather not learn KDP’s settings yourself, a <a href="/services/publishing-services">publishing service</a> can handle the upload, metadata and listing for you, as long as the book stays on your own account.</p>
<p>The real question, then, isn’t “what does Amazon charge?” It’s <strong>“what does it cost to make a book people want to buy?”</strong> That’s where the budget goes.</p>

<h2 id="breakdown">The 7 costs of self-publishing a book</h2>

<h3 id="editing">1. Book editing: $0 to $5,000+</h3>
<p>Editing is usually the largest line in a self-publishing budget, and the one with the biggest effect on reviews. Readers forgive a plain cover more easily than a book full of errors. There are three levels, and most books don’t need all of them:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Developmental editing</strong> looks at the big picture: structure, pacing, plot or argument, character, gaps. Typical range: about <strong>$0.03–$0.08 per word</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Copyediting / line editing</strong> fixes clarity, flow, consistency and grammar sentence by sentence. Typical range: about <strong>$0.02–$0.05 per word</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Proofreading</strong> is the final check for typos and formatting slips before publication. Typical range: about <strong>$0.01–$0.03 per word</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="bp-fig"><img src="/assets/img/lp/editing.webp" alt="An editor marking up a printed manuscript with a red pen beside an editing checklist" width="1254" height="1254" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Editing is usually the largest line in a self-publishing budget, and the one readers notice most.</figcaption></figure>
<p>For a 60,000-word book, that works out to roughly <strong>$1,800–$4,800</strong> for a developmental edit, <strong>$1,200–$3,000</strong> for a copyedit and <strong>$600–$1,800</strong> for a proofread.</p>
<div class="bp-tip"><p><strong>Where to save:</strong> If beta readers aren’t confused by the story or argument, skip the developmental edit and invest in a copyedit or a professional proofread. A clean proofread is the minimum for any book you plan to sell.</p></div>
<p>Our own <a href="/services/proofreading-services">proofreading starts at $180</a> and <a href="/services/book-editing-services">book editing at $600</a>, with a written quote based on your word count.</p>

<h3 id="cover">2. Book cover design: $0 to $1,500</h3>
<p>Your cover is your book’s advertisement. On Amazon it first appears as a thumbnail about the size of a postage stamp, beside dozens of competing titles. It has to signal the genre instantly and stay readable when tiny.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>DIY or template:</strong> free to about $50. Fine for a first draft of your vision, rarely good enough to compete.</li>
  <li><strong>Premade cover:</strong> roughly $50–$200. A finished design with your title added; quick and affordable, but not unique.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom ebook cover:</strong> roughly $200–$600.</li>
  <li><strong>Full print wrap</strong> (front, spine and back for a paperback): usually an extra $100–$300, because the spine width depends on your final page count.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="bp-fig"><img src="/assets/img/lp/showcase.webp" alt="Two professionally designed book covers shown as 3D paperbacks" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A genre-right cover has to work as a tiny Amazon thumbnail and as a full print wrap.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Our <a href="/services/book-cover-design-services">book cover design starts at $250</a>, and every publishing package includes a free 3D cover.</p>

<h3 id="formatting">3. Formatting and typesetting: $0 to $800</h3>
<p>Formatting turns your manuscript into files that read well on a Kindle and print correctly as a paperback: chapter headings, margins, trim size, page numbers, a working table of contents.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Free:</strong> Amazon’s Kindle Create handles simple, text-only books well.</li>
  <li><strong>Software:</strong> tools like Vellum (Mac only, a one-time license of roughly $200–$250) produce polished ebooks and print interiors.</li>
  <li><strong>Professional formatting:</strong> about $100–$300 for a standard novel or nonfiction book, more for books with images, tables, footnotes or fixed layouts.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/book-formatting-services">book formatting starts at $150</a> and covers Kindle, EPUB and print-ready files.</p>

<h3 id="isbn">4. ISBN and barcode: $0 to $295</h3>
<p>An ISBN identifies a specific edition of your book. You have two options:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Free KDP ISBN:</strong> fine for most authors who publish their paperback on Amazon. The catch: Amazon’s imprint is listed as the publisher, and that ISBN can’t be used with other printers.</li>
  <li><strong>Your own ISBN:</strong> in the US, ISBNs are sold by <a href="https://www.myidentifiers.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bowker</a> at <strong>$125 for one or $295 for ten</strong>. Each format (paperback, hardcover, audiobook) needs its own, which is why the 10-pack is better value.</li>
</ul>
<p>Kindle ebooks don’t need an ISBN at all. Amazon assigns its own identifier (ASIN).</p>

<h3 id="printing">5. Printing: $0 upfront</h3>
<p>With print-on-demand you never pay a printer upfront. Amazon prints each paperback when it’s ordered and subtracts the printing cost from your royalty. According to KDP’s <a href="https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G201834340" target="_blank" rel="noopener">printing cost guidelines</a>, a black-ink paperback is priced as a fixed cost plus a per-page cost. On Amazon.com that’s roughly <strong>$1.00 + $0.012 per page</strong> for books over 108 pages, so:</p>
<ul>
  <li>a 200-page paperback costs about <strong>$3.40</strong> to print</li>
  <li>a 300-page paperback costs about <strong>$4.60</strong> to print</li>
</ul>
<p>The only printing you pay for directly is <strong>author copies</strong>, which you can order at print cost plus shipping for events, giveaways and reviewers. Color interiors cost considerably more per page, so check KDP’s calculator before choosing color.</p>

<h3 id="marketing">6. Marketing and launch: $0 to $3,000+</h3>
<p>A book nobody can find doesn’t sell, however good it is. Marketing is the most flexible line in the budget, and the one where spending without a plan wastes the most money.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Free foundations:</strong> a compelling book description, the right categories and keywords, an Amazon Author Central page, and a list of advance readers who will leave honest reviews.</li>
  <li><strong>Amazon Ads:</strong> you set the budget, and many authors start with a few dollars a day to test keywords before scaling what works.</li>
  <li><strong>Author website and email list:</strong> the asset that lets you sell your next book without paying for every reader again.</li>
  <li><strong>Professional launch help:</strong> our <a href="/services/book-marketing-services">book marketing starts at $500</a>.</li>
</ul>
<div class="bp-tip"><p><strong>Where to save:</strong> Fix the listing before you buy traffic. Ads sent to a weak cover or a vague description burn money. Get the cover, description and categories right first, then test ads with a small daily budget.</p></div>

<h3 id="optional">7. Optional extras</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Illustrations</strong> for children’s books: often the largest cost in that genre, from a few hundred to several thousand dollars.</li>
  <li><strong>Audiobook production:</strong> narration, editing and mastering, usually priced per finished hour.</li>
  <li><strong>Copyright registration</strong> with the U.S. Copyright Office: a modest online filing fee (check <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">copyright.gov</a> for the current amount). Your work is protected by copyright as soon as it’s written; registration strengthens your position if you ever need to enforce it.</li>
  <li><strong>Ghostwriting</strong>, if someone else writes the book: a different budget altogether, typically several thousand dollars and up.</li>
</ul>

