<h2 id="royalties">Royalties: how many copies to break even?</h2>
<p>Spending money only makes sense next to what the book can earn. Here’s how Amazon pays you, using the figures KDP publishes.</p>

<h3 id="ebook-royalties">Ebook royalties</h3>
<p>Kindle ebooks earn either a <strong>35% or 70% royalty</strong>. The 70% option applies when your price falls within Amazon’s eligible price band (it starts at $2.99 on Amazon.com; check <a href="https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G200644210" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KDP’s ebook royalty page</a> for the current upper limit) and includes a small delivery fee based on file size. A $4.99 text-only ebook earns roughly <strong>$3.40 per sale</strong>.</p>

<h3 id="paperback-royalties">Paperback royalties</h3>
<p>Paperbacks earn <strong>60% of the list price minus the printing cost</strong> when priced at $9.99 or more on Amazon.com. Take a 250-page paperback priced at $14.99:</p>
<div class="bp-math">
  <p><span>List price</span><b>$14.99</b></p>
  <p><span>60% royalty rate</span><b>$8.99</b></p>
  <p><span>Printing cost ($1.00 + 250 × $0.012)</span><b>− $4.00</b></p>
  <p class="t"><span>Your royalty per copy</span><b>≈ $4.99</b></p>
</div>

<h3 id="break-even">Break-even examples</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A <strong>$750 lean budget</strong> breaks even at about <strong>150 paperbacks</strong> or about <strong>220 ebooks</strong>.</li>
  <li>A <strong>$1,500 professional budget</strong> breaks even at about <strong>300 paperbacks</strong> or <strong>440 ebooks</strong>.</li>
  <li>Most authors sell a mix, and ebooks and paperbacks together reach break-even faster than either alone.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is why the cover and the first pages matter so much: they’re what turn a browser into one of those copies. It’s also why many authors plan a second book early. A reader who enjoys book one is the cheapest reader you’ll ever find for book two.</p>

<h2 id="save-money">7 ways to cut costs without cutting corners</h2>
<ol class="bp-ol">
  <li><strong>Get a proofread, always.</strong> If you can only pay for one service, make it this one.</li>
  <li><strong>Use beta readers before a developmental edit.</strong> Five honest readers can tell you whether the structure works before you pay someone to fix it.</li>
  <li><strong>Edit before you pay an editor.</strong> Every self-editing pass shortens a paid edit, and many editors charge less for clean manuscripts.</li>
  <li><strong>Spend on the cover, save on the interior.</strong> Free formatting tools are good for text-only books; a weak cover costs sales on every impression.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the free KDP ISBN</strong> unless you need your own imprint or wide print distribution.</li>
  <li><strong>Launch organically first.</strong> Advance readers, a strong description and the right categories cost nothing. Test ads only once the listing converts.</li>
  <li><strong>Compare bundles with à-la-carte prices.</strong> A package that includes editing, cover, formatting and publishing often costs less than hiring each specialist separately.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="red-flags">Red flags: costs you should never pay</h2>
<p>Self-publishing services range from excellent to predatory. Before you sign, check that the company does <strong>not</strong>:</p>
<ul class="bp-flags">
  <li>take a <strong>percentage of your royalties</strong>, now or later</li>
  <li>publish your book on <strong>their account</strong> instead of yours</li>
  <li>keep the <strong>rights or the final files</strong> after you’ve paid</li>
  <li>charge <strong>thousands upfront</strong> for packages of vague “marketing” with no clear deliverables</li>
  <li>promise <strong>bestseller status</strong> or guaranteed sales numbers</li>
</ul>
<p>A trustworthy service gives you a written quote, publishes under your name on accounts you control, and hands over every file. That’s exactly how we work: <strong>you keep 100% of your rights and royalties.</strong></p>

